      "def:selector-transition",
      "def:fixing-transition"
    ],
    "justification": "The proved simulation establishes that the fixed input-determined compiler retains every support-licensed successful formula of the anchor algorithm.",
    "gap": "This closes the positive preservation layer through fixed-support normalization but deliberately leaves the residual completeness conjecture untouched.",
    "consumer": "Existing systematic-selection analyses can replay the normalized certificate for a valid formula without changing the estimand or adding an undeployed selector context.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix a successful published run satisfying the two support hypotheses. By \\(\\textnormal{lem:published-trace-scheduling}\\), the input-determined compiler follows a dependency-topological simulation of that run, never enters a residual state before its successful terminal call, and then presents the same reduced circuit \\(F_H=P_H/D_H\\) to the projection verifier. Its exact accumulated identity is\n\\[\n D_H\\,t_{a,H}^N-P_H(o_H^N)=0\\pmod{\\mathcal J}.\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\nEvery factor of \\(D_H\\) is a context normalizer, reachable fixing margin, or reduced elimination denominator paired with the corresponding ledger factorization. The hypothesis that the published run uses only contexts in \\(H\\) ensures that every initial normalizer is\n\\[\n p_H(S=s)=\n \\sum_{v:(v,s)\\in\\mathcal C_H^{\\mathrm{adm}}(G^+)}p_H(v,s),\n \\qquad s\\in H.\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\nBy \\(\\textnormal{ass:admissible-cell-interior}\\), (19) is a nonempty sum of strictly positive cells. The inductive certificates in \\(\\textnormal{lem:nonresidual-progress}\\), together with \\(\\textnormal{lem:restricted-support-denominator-positivity}\\), give\n\\[\n d_{H,r}\\{O_H(\\theta)\\}>0\n \\quad\\text{for every registered }r\n \\quad\\text{and every }\\theta\\in\\Theta_H.\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\nThe definition of the branch domain and (20) imply\n\\[\n O_H(\\Theta_H)\\subseteq\\mathfrak D_H.\n\\tag{21}\n\\]\n\nOn this domain \\(D_H>0\\), so division in (18) is legitimate and yields\n\\[\n t_{a,H}^N(\\eta)\n =F_H\\{O_H^N(\\eta)\\}\n \\qquad(\\eta\\in\\mathcal I_H).\n\\tag{22}\n\\]\nFor \\(\\theta\\in\\Theta_H\\), put \\(\\eta=\\nu_H(\\theta)\\). The normalization equivalence theorem gives\n\\[\n O_H^N\\{\\nu_H(\\theta)\\}=O_H(\\theta),\n \\qquad\n T_{a,H}^N\\{\\nu_H(\\theta)\\}=T_{a,H}(\\theta).\n\\tag{23}\n\\]\nSubstitution of (23) into (22) proves\n\\[\n F_H\\{O_H(\\theta)\\}=T_{a,H}(\\theta)\n \\qquad(\\theta\\in\\Theta_H).\n\\tag{24}\n\\]\nFinally, the scheduling lemma proves operation-by-operation equality, modulo the stored gate and retained-image equations, of the normalized labelled kernels and the published kernels after gate reinsertion. Equations (19)--(20) identify their required positive margins as well. This is the asserted algebraic preservation on the original expansion, and no selector value outside \\(H\\) has been introduced.",
